item.content:encoded:
A reconciliation-first approach to CDC into Redshift, and where GoldenGate and AWS DMS pipelines actually go wrong.Cutover night. The row counts match to the last digit. Somebody posts a green checkmark in the channel and everyone goes to bed.Three days later, finance asks why order totals for one region are off by a few thousand dollars. Nothing is down. Nothing alerted. The counts still match. That’s the problem.The pattern is familiar to anyone who has moved a busy database onto a new platform. The pipeline gets built, the full load finishes, change data capture (CDC) hums along, and the team picks the one check that’s easy to run: count the rows. Counts catch missing data. They say nothing about data that arrived wrong, arrived twice, or never got its update.This article is about the checks I’d automate before trusting either GoldenGate or AWS DMS to feed Redshift, and the lag arithmetic that tells you when the pipeline is falling behind.Treat row counts as a smoke test, never as the exit criterion for a cutover.Land every change as an append-only record, then apply the latest change per key. Don’t rely on Redshift to reject duplicates.Alert on the slope of lag, and put source-side bulk jobs on a calendar.Reconcile with bucketed checksums over the whole table, then drill into the buckets that disagree.Every number below comes from one worked example with made-up data: an Oracle ORDERS table of 480 million rows being replicated into Redshift. It is illustrative, not from a real system, and I flag it each time.1. Row counts are a smoke testA matching count proves the pipeline didn’t lose or duplicate whole rows at one moment in time. It’s a useful first check, and I run it. But look at what it can’t see:An update was lost or applied out of orderThe row exists on both sidesA delete never reached the targetCounts drift, but only after enough deletesA large text column was truncated in flightSame keys, shorter valuesAn empty string became NULL (or the reverse)Timestamps lost precision or shifted time zoneSame keys, different valuesThe same change was applied twiceFive of those six defects leave the count untouched or only occasionally move it. If your exit criterion is “counts match,” you’ve certified the easiest thing to get right.People compare GoldenGate and DMS on feature lists. I compare them on what happens when something goes wrong, because that’s where the weekends go. This is my rule of thumb, not a law. Check licensing and the current documentation for your versions before you decide.Extract reads the redo, writes trail filesA task on a replication instance reads the source logsTrail files absorb the backlog (size the disk)Source logs must stay available until the task catches upRich mapping and filteringTable mappings and basic rulesYou run and upgrade the processesManaged service, fewer moving partsHigh-volume Oracle estates, strict ordering, long-lived replicationTime-boxed migrations, small teams, AWS-native stacksWhichever you choose, the target side looks the same, and that’s where most of this article lives.3. Five stages, three checksThe pipeline is easy to draw. The discipline is in giving each stage a check that can fail loudly: Is the backlog growing or shrinking? (Section 5.) Does the number of changes landed per batch match what the source log says it emitted? Does the content agree, not just the count? (Section 6.)4. Land the changes, then apply the latest per keyI land every change as an append-only record with its operation, commit position and sequence. Then I apply only the latest change per key. Two reasons. First, retries and replays are harmless, because applying the same batch twice gives the same result. Second, Redshift doesn’t enforce primary keys. They’re informational, used by the planner, so the target will happily store duplicates and won’t tell you.Untested sketch, Redshift SQL (check syntax against your cluster version; newer clusters also support MERGE):CREATE TABLE orders_changes ( batch_id BIGINT, op CHAR(1), commit_scn BIGINT, seq BIGINT, order_id BIGINT, status VARCHAR(20), amount_cents BIGINT, updated_at TIMESTAMP );
CREATE TEMP TABLE latest AS SELECT * FROM ( SELECT c.*, ROW_NUMBER() OVER ( PARTITION BY order_id ORDER BY commit_scn DESC, seq DESC) AS rn FROM orders_changes c WHERE batch_id = 1042 ) AS t WHERE rn = 1;
BEGIN; DELETE FROM orders USING latest l WHERE orders.order_id = l.order_id; INSERT INTO orders SELECT order_id, status, amount_cents, updated_at FROM latest WHERE op <> 'D'; COMMIT; Deletes arrive as a change record, so the DELETE removes the old row and the INSERT skips it. Updates become delete-plus-insert, which suits a columnar store far better than row-by-row updates. In the worked example, 12 million changes a day split into 6.6 million inserts, 4.8 million updates and 0.6 million deletes (55%, 40% and 5%). Batching those is not optional.5. Alert on the slope, not the levelAverage lag is a comfortable lie. Here’s the arithmetic for the example. The table sees 12 million changes a day, about 139 a second on average and roughly 1,100 a second in the busiest hour (I assumed an 8x peak). Suppose the apply side can sustain 3,000 changes a second. That’s 2.7 times the peak. Healthy.Now somebody runs a backfill that updates 40 million rows in an hour. That adds about 11,100 changes a second, for a total near 11,250 against capacity of 3,000. The surplus is 8,250 a second.Using lag ≈ backlog ÷ apply rate as a rough estimate, a 5-minute target is breached after about 109 seconds, because the backlog passes 900,000 changes. By the end of the job the backlog is 29.7 million and lag peaks near 165 minutes. Draining it at the 2,861 a second of headroom takes another 173 minutes, so the pipeline is back to normal about 293 minutes after the job started.A backfill that takes an hour at the source can cost you five hours of staleness downstream.Two habits fix most of this. Alert when lag is rising for ten minutes, not when it crosses a line, so you hear about it early. And put source-side bulk jobs on a shared calendar, so the pipeline owner knows a surge is coming before the pager does. In DMS, watch the source and target latency metrics separately; they point to different bottlenecks (check the troubleshooting guide for your version).6. Reconcile with checksums, not samplesThe usual response to “counts aren’t enough” is to compare a random sample of rows. It feels rigorous. It isn’t, and the maths is short. If N is the table size, n the sample size and k the number of corrupted rows, the chance of catching at least one is about 1 − (1 − n/N)^k.In the example, 480 million rows and 120 bad ones (0.000025% of the table). A 1-million-row sample finds at least one bad row 22% of the time. A 10-million-row sample finds one 92% of the time, and costs ten times as much. Checksums over the whole table find it every time, because every row is counted in some bucket.So I bucket the keys, compute cheap aggregates per bucket on both sides, and diff the results. Here’s a query that runs unchanged on Oracle and Redshift (untested; adapt types to your schema):SELECT MOD(order_id, 4096) AS bucket, COUNT(*) AS n, SUM(amount_cents) AS amt, SUM(CASE status WHEN 'NEW' THEN 1 WHEN 'PAID' THEN 2 WHEN 'SHIPPED' THEN 3 ELSE 0 END) AS st, MAX(updated_at) AS max_upd FROM orders WHERE updated_at <= :cutoff GROUP BY MOD(order_id, 4096); With 4,096 buckets, each holds about 117,000 rows. The 120 bad rows can touch at most 120 buckets, so the drill-down scans at most 14 million rows, 2.9% of the table. Two details matter more than the SQL:Same cut-off on both sides. Compare the source as of a commit point and the target after it has applied everything up to that point. Otherwise in-flight changes show up as false mismatches and the team learns to ignore the report. Hash functions differ between engines, so I compare counts, sums and maxima on normalised columns. For text columns, canonicalise first: trim, case, and above all the empty-string question (see the pitfalls).7. A cutover runbook with gatesThe template I’d steal: each step has an action and a gate, and the gate is a number somebody can check. “Three clean reconciliation nights in a row” beats “looks good.” The T+7 row matters too. Keep the reverse path warm for a week, because mismatches that survive three clean nights tend to be the slow ones: month-end jobs, rare code paths, the table nobody remembered.8. Where I’d bet you’ll tripEmpty string versus NULL. Oracle treats an empty string as NULL. Redshift doesn’t. Your counts match and your WHERE clauses disagree. Check how your tool treats LOB columns. Some modes cap the size and truncate silently, which is exactly the defect counts can’t see. If the source purges its logs before the pipeline reads them, you’re back to a full reload. Set the retention for the worst outage you can imagine, not the average week.Schema changes mid-stream. A column added on the source on Thursday breaks something on Friday. Freeze DDL before cutover and test one change in rehearsal. Precision, time zones and daylight-saving boundaries. Compare them explicitly; they rarely fail loudly.No owner for reconciliation. A report nobody reads is the same as no report. Give it a name, a schedule and a threshold that pages someone.Counts are a smoke test. Land changes append-only and apply the latest per key. Watch the slope of lag and know your bulk jobs. Reconcile with bucketed checksums at a shared cut-off, and give every cutover step a gate that is a number.

In late April 2026, a coding agent working for a small software company called PocketOS ran into a credential error in a staging environment. It didn't stop to ask anyone. It searched the project, found an API token in a file unrelated to its task, and used that token to delete a storage volume on Railway, the company's cloud host. The volume held the production database, and the backups sat on the same volume. The deletion took about nine seconds.Founder Jer Crane wrote afterwards that the token had been created for routine domain operations through Railway's command line tool. Nobody at PocketOS knew it could also delete production volumes. The company restored from a backup that was three months old, then rebuilt the missing bookings from payment histories, calendar entries and customer emails.Nothing in that story was a hack. The token was valid and the API call was well formed, so Railway did what the credential allowed. Every system in the chain was asked "can this caller do this?" and each one correctly answered yes. No system asked who had decided the agent should be doing it.Security people keep two words for this. A permission is what a credential can technically do. Authority is the right to do it, and it has to come from someone entitled to give it. Most agents running today have plenty of the first and a thin paper trail for the second. This piece looks at how that happens and what a proper answer looks like.Three questions that usually get asked as oneAuthentication answers "who is this?" A password, a passkey or a signed certificate proves the caller is who it claims to be. It says nothing about what the caller should be allowed to do.Authorisation answers "what may this caller do?" Roles, scopes and policies live here. A token that can read your calendar but can't delete events is an authorisation decision.Delegation answers a question the first two skip: on whose behalf is this caller acting, and within what limits?If you have ever sent someone to collect a passport or a bank document for you, you already know what good delegation looks like. You write an authority letter. It names you, names the person you are sending, says what they may collect, and carries a date. The clerk checks the bearer's ID against the letter. Your cousin walks out with one envelope, and he can't empty your account while he's there.Software has mostly handled the same errand in two worse ways. Either the cousin gets your own ID card and the clerk believes he is you, or he gets a master key to the building. The first is impersonation. The second is a broad API key. In both cases the clerk has no letter to check.RFC 8693, the OAuth token exchange standard from January 2020, draws this line formally. Under impersonation, the acting party is indistinguishable from the person it acts for. Under delegation, it keeps its own identity and acts as that person's agent. The word "agent" was in the RFC years before anyone attached a language model to it.Why this matters more for agents than for scriptsA nightly cron job with an over-powered key was always a risk, but a predictable one. Its code did the same thing every run, and you could read that code.An agent decides what to do at run time. Part of what shapes the decision is text it picks up along the way: a web page, an email, a GitHub issue, an error message. Auth0's identity team puts it this way: once behaviour is no longer fixed by code, the scope of the credential is one of the few controls you have left.How agents usually get their accessThe first time you connect an agent to a database or a cloud account, the quickest route is the credential you already have. That is usually your own, or an admin key sitting in a config file. Four patterns cover almost every setup.A Cloud Security Alliance survey from January 2026 gives a rough idea of the spread. Of 228 IT and security professionals, 43% said their agents rely on shared service accounts and 31% let agents run under human user identities. 74% said agents often get more access than they need. 68% could not clearly tell agent activity from human activity in their own systems.The sample is small and the study was funded by Aembit, which sells agent identity products, so treat the figures as a sketch. The shape is still familiar. PocketOS fits the first row of the table: a key made for one job that turned out to work for every job.Standards people have noticed. An Internet-Draft on agent authentication, written by engineers from Okta, Ping Identity, AWS, OpenAI, Zscaler and Defakto Security, calls static API keys an antipattern for agents. Its reasoning is that they are long-lived bearer secrets, bound to nothing, and hard to rotate. The document is a draft and may change, but its authors include Brian Campbell, who edited RFC 8693.The confused deputy, 38 years onIn 1988 Norm Hardy published a three-page note called The Confused Deputy. It described a compiler on a commercial timesharing system. The compiler had write access to its home directory so it could record usage statistics. The system's billing file lived in the same directory.One day a user told the compiler to write its debugging output to a file with the billing file's name. The compiler obliged, and the billing records were overwritten. The user had no right to touch that file. The compiler did, and it couldn't tell whose authority it was using for that particular write.An AI agent is Hardy's compiler with a much larger vocabulary. It carries credentials from whoever deployed it, and it takes direction from whatever text lands in its context window.Researchers at Invariant Labs showed in 2025 that a malicious issue filed on a GitHub repository could hijack an agent connected through the official GitHub MCP server. The attacker needed no access to the victim's private repositories. The agent already had it.Not every failure needs an attacker, though. In mid-2025, Replit's coding agent deleted a user's production database after being told not to change anything without permission. The PocketOS agent wasn't tricked either. On its own account afterwards, it guessed that the volume belonged to staging and never checked.The same control stops both kinds. If the credential can't perform the damaging action, it doesn't matter whether the agent was fooled or just wrong.What properly delegated access looks likeVery little of the fix is new. Most of it is OAuth plumbing that has existed for years and is now being pointed at agents. Six pieces do the work.The agent has its own identity. The IETF draft treats an agent as a workload with one unique identifier and short-lived credentials cryptographically bound to it. It also says the model itself must never see those credentials, so a prompt injection has nothing to leak.A person grants a narrow slice. The user signs in and approves a consent screen that names the agent and the specific access it wants. This is the authority letter being written.The token carries both names. After a token exchange, the access token lists the user as the subject and the agent as the actor.The token works in one place only. The MCP authorisation rules require clients to say which server a token is for, and require servers to reject any token that wasn't issued to them. Forwarding a client's token to another API is forbidden outright.Tokens are short-lived and fetched per task. The agent never holds a long-lived refresh token. A broker holds it and hands out a fresh, narrow token for each operation. Revoke the connection at the identity provider and no new tokens are issued.Scopes start small. MCP's security guidance recommends a minimal read-only baseline, with extra scopes requested only when a privileged action is first attempted. It warns against wildcard scopes and against bundling unrelated privileges to avoid future prompts.Here is what step 3 looks like in practice. This is the payload of a delegated token for an invented scheduling agent:{
  "sub": "meera@example.com",
  "aud": "https://calendar.example.com",
  "scope": "calendar.events.read",
  "exp": 1794445200,
  "act": { "sub": "agent:scheduling-assistant" }
}
Five fields answer the question in this article's title. sub says whose authority this is. act says who is using it. aud and scope say where and for what, and exp says until when. The calendar API can now apply a rule to the pair: Meera may delete events, but the scheduling agent acting for Meera may only read them. The audit log records both names.Where this is still unfinishedIt would be dishonest to present this as solved. Several separate Internet-Drafts on agent authorisation were circulating at the IETF in 2026, and none is a standard yet.Scopes are also blunt. Auth0 points out that OAuth scopes can't easily express something like "contact details for the accounts I'm working on, but not the legal hold queue". Rules that fine need a policy check on every request, on top of the token.And none of this helps if a second, broader credential is lying in a file the agent can read. That was the entire PocketOS failure.An "Are you sure?" box is not authorisationMany agent tools show a confirmation prompt before a risky step. That is useful, but it is weaker than it looks, for two reasons.The prompt is drawn by the same software it is supposed to restrain. The IETF draft is blunt about this. A confirmation inside the agent's own interface does not count as authorisation unless it is tied to a grant from the authorisation server. The stronger pattern sends the approval request out of band, to the user's phone for example, with the details of the exact action attached. The draft admits that approvals in the middle of a running task still need more standards work.The second reason is that words are not controls. The Replit user had told the agent not to make changes without permission. PocketOS had rules written into the agent's instructions. Telling an agent to stay out of production is a request. Removing its write access to production is a control, and only one of those survives a bad guess.There is a human limit as well. Ask someone to approve every file read and by the afternoon they are approving everything. MCP's guidance lists users abandoning consent dialogs that ask for too much as a risk in its own right. Approval works when it is rare. Keep it for actions you can't take back: deleting data, moving money, sending something outside the company, changing who has access.Seven questions before you hand an agent accessYou don't need an identity platform to get most of the benefit. You need honest answers to these.Does the agent have its own identity, or is it borrowing mine or a shared one?If I read the audit log six months from now, will it show the agent, the person it acted for, and the action?What is the worst single call this credential can make? Find out before the agent does.When does this access end if I do nothing at all?Can I revoke it in one place, today, without breaking unrelated things?Which actions need a human yes, and is that yes enforced by the system or only by the prompt?What other credentials can the agent read from disk or from environment variables?The last question is the one PocketOS missed, and it is the easiest to miss. An agent with shell access can read every secret in the working directory, whether or not you meant to hand it over. For a solo developer or a small team, the practical version is short. Give the agent a per-project token instead of an account-wide one. Point it at a read-only database user unless the task needs writes. Keep production secrets out of the folder it works in. Store backups somewhere the same credential can't reach.What is the difference between authentication and authorisation for an AI agent?Authentication proves which agent is calling. Authorisation decides what that agent may do. An agent can be perfectly authenticated and still hold far more access than its task needs.Should an AI agent log in as me?Preferably not. If the agent uses your login, every system treats its actions as yours and the logs can't tell the two of you apart. A delegated token that names both you and the agent keeps the record straight and lets you limit what the agent can do.What does "on behalf of" mean in OAuth?It describes a token that records two parties: the user whose authority is being used and the actor using it. RFC 8693 defines the token exchange that produces these tokens and the act claim that names the actor.Does MCP take care of authorisation for me?Partly. The MCP specification defines how a client gets an OAuth token for a remote server, ties that token to that one server and bans passing it on. It does not choose your scopes, and it can't stop a local agent from reading a broader key off your disk.

Most years PostgreSQL ships a new release in September, but this year's release came two months later thanks mainly to the flurry of issues that the PostgreSQL team needed to address as part of the ongoing AI-reported vulnerability apocalypse.This release was definitely worth the wait. My favorite new feature is this one:For applications that send reads to standbys, PostgreSQL 19 adds "read-your-writes" consistency with the new  command. A session on a standby can use  to wait until the standby has replayed the write-ahead log (WAL) up to a specific log sequence number (LSN) before running a query, so the application can read data it just wrote on the primary.In practice, that looks like this:UPDATE movie SET genre = 'Dramatic' WHERE genre = 'Drama';
SELECT pg_current_wal_insert_lsn();
That returns a value like . Store that somewhere (a signed cookie would work) such that on a subsequent request your application can do:WAIT FOR LSN '0/0306EE20' WITH (TIMEOUT '0.1s');
SELECT * FROM movie WHERE genre = 'Drama';
The timeout is optional - if you provide one you'll get an error if the replica you are talking to doesn't catch up within the specified time.I first encountered this pattern in Wikipedia, which has a hand-rolled version of this built against MySQL replication:In addition to this, MediaWiki attempts to ensure that the user sees events occurring on the wiki in chronological order. A few seconds of lag can be tolerated, as long as the user sees a consistent picture from subsequent requests. This is done by saving the primary binlog position in the session, and then at the start of each request, waiting for the replica to catch up to that position before doing any reads from it.

